    {
      "id": "t015235",
      "source": "Map scale links a model to the real world through proportional reasoning.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t015236",
      "source": "Given facts.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t015237",
      "source": "A map has scale 1:10000. The measured map distance between two points is 6 cm. Rule: 1 cm on the map represents 10000 cm in reality; 100,000 cm = 1 km.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t015238",
      "source": "Rules.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t015239",
      "source": "Multiply map length by the scale denominator, then convert units. A scale is a ratio, not an added distance.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t015240",
      "source": "Task.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t015241",
      "source": "What is the real straight-line distance in kilometres?",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t015242",
      "source": "Step-by-step solution.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t015243",
      "source": "1. Real centimetres=6×10000=60000. 2. Convert to kilometres: 60000÷100,000=0.6.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t015244",
      "source": "Answer.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t015245",
      "source": "0.6 km.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t015246",
      "source": "Knowledge takeaway.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t015247",
      "source": "Scale problems are ratio problems: keep units explicit and conversion becomes mechanical.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t015248",
      "source": "Formal model.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t015249",
      "source": "Proportion d_real=d_map×denominator, followed by unit conversion.",
      "translation": ""
    },
